# Line endings, normalized to LF everywhere — including Windows working copies.
#
# This exists because CI went red for five consecutive runs and the cause was the absence of
# this file. The situation was:
#
#   * no .gitattributes, and core.autocrlf=true on the Windows dev machine
#   * so the repository stores LF (autocrlf converts on commit) while the Windows working tree
#     holds CRLF (autocrlf converts on checkout)
#   * .editorconfig was then written with `end_of_line = crlf`, read off the working copy
#   * `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` passed locally (CRLF tree, CRLF rule) and failed on the
#     Linux runner with 39,380 ENDOFLINE errors (LF tree, CRLF rule)
#
# Local and CI could not agree, by construction, and no amount of re-running the formatter on
# Windows would ever have revealed it. `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree on every platform,
# so the tree, the repository and both formatters now see the same bytes. .editorconfig says
# `end_of_line = lf` to match; change the two together or not at all.
#
# A practical side effect worth having: the patch scripts used to edit files here write LF, and
# so does every Linux tool. Those no longer produce a diff against the working tree.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF regardless — these run on the Pi.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binaries: never touch the bytes. `text=auto` detects most of these, but being explicit means a
# corrupted font or screenshot can never be blamed on line-ending conversion.
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.gz   binary
*.zst  binary
*.zip  binary
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.kml  -text
